{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Transitive Game Balance.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Transitive Game Balance.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"transitive game balance transitive game balance","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Transitive Game Balance.md","fileName":"Transitive Game Balance.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Transitive Game Balance","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 14:36\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Game Balance and Progression]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Balance and Progression","advancedgamedesign.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Transitive Game Balance.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Transitive Game Balance.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"transitive game balance transitive game balance","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Transitive Game Balance.md","fileName":"Transitive Game Balance.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Transitive Game Balance","heading":"Transitive Game Balance","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"In the source's terminology, transitive game balance is a circular relationship in which each type defeats some peers and is defeated by others, as in rock-paper-scissors. No one part dominates the whole system.\n\nThe structure uses an odd number of peer types so each can have a clear advantage over half and disadvantage against half of the others. External factors must still perturb the ecology enough to preserve strategy without producing a dominant path.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Balance and Progression","advancedgamedesign.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Transitive Game Balance.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Transitive Game Balance.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"transitive ga